course: space
lesson: 7
title: Phases, eclipses and tides
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Sun always lights half of the Moon, like a lamp lighting half of a ball; as the Moon goes round us we see more or less of that lit half, and these are the phases.
explain: The phases are not the Earth's shadow; the Earth's shadow touches the Moon only in a lunar eclipse, which happens at full Moon and can turn it coppery red.
explain: In a solar eclipse the Moon slides between us and the Sun at new Moon and covers it; because the Moon's path is tilted, this does not happen every month.
explain: The Moon's gravity also tugs the oceans into bulges, so the sea goes in and out twice a day, and at new and full Moon the Sun helps pull to make the biggest spring tides.
explain: Never look at a solar eclipse with bare eyes or ordinary sunglasses; use proper eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector.
fact: The Moon's phases happen because we see different amounts of its sunlit half as it orbits the Earth.
fact: The cycle from new Moon through first quarter, full Moon and last quarter back to new Moon takes about 29.5 days.
fact: A growing Moon is waxing and a shrinking one is waning, and from Britain a waxing Moon is lit on its right side.
fact: The phases are not caused by the Earth's shadow; this is a common mistake.
fact: A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth and blocks the Sun's light, which can happen only at new Moon.
fact: A lunar eclipse happens at full Moon when the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, often turning it a coppery red.
fact: The red colour comes from sunlight bent through the Earth's atmosphere onto the Moon, like all the world's sunsets at once.
fact: We do not get eclipses every month because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees to the Earth's orbit.
fact: You must never look at a solar eclipse with your bare eyes or ordinary sunglasses, only with proper eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector.
fact: The oceans have two high tides and two low tides roughly every day, caused mainly by the Moon's gravity.
fact: Spring tides, with the biggest range, happen at new and full Moon, and neap tides, with the smallest range, at the quarter Moons.
fact: Spring tides have nothing to do with the season of spring; they happen all year round.
fact: The Bristol Channel and Canada's Bay of Fundy have some of the biggest tides in the world, over 12 metres.
exercise: Why does the Moon have phases? | We see different amounts of its sunlit half as it orbits | The Earth's shadow slowly covers a bigger part of it each night | Clouds hide part of the Moon | The Moon changes its shape every night
exercise: How long is the cycle of the Moon's phases? | About 29.5 days | About 7 days, one week | About 365 days, a year | About 24 hours, one day
exercise: Which order is right? | New Moon, first quarter, full Moon, last quarter | Full Moon, new Moon, first quarter, last quarter | First quarter, last quarter, new Moon, full Moon | New Moon, full Moon, first quarter, last quarter
exercise: What is a waxing Moon? | A Moon that is growing, showing more each night | A Moon that is shrinking each night | A Moon covered by the Earth's shadow | A Moon that shines with its own light, like a lamp
exercise: Seen from Britain, which side of a waxing Moon is lit? | The right side | The left side | The top only | The bottom only
exercise: What happens in a solar eclipse? | The Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth and blocks the Sun | The Earth's shadow falls on the Moon | The Sun goes behind a cloud of dust | The Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon
exercise: At which phase can a solar eclipse happen? | New Moon | Full Moon | First quarter | Last quarter
exercise: What happens in a lunar eclipse? | The Earth's shadow falls on the Moon | The Moon blocks out the Sun in the daytime | The Sun's light goes out briefly for a minute | The Moon goes round behind the Sun for a while
exercise: What colour can the Moon turn in a lunar eclipse? | A coppery red | A bright green | A pure, shining white | A deep ocean blue
exercise: Why don't we get eclipses every month? | The Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees to the Earth's | The Moon is too small most months | The Sun moves out of the way of the Moon most months, by chance | Clouds hide most eclipses
exercise: How may you safely watch a solar eclipse? | With proper eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector | With ordinary sunglasses | With bare eyes for a few seconds | Through binoculars without any filter, just for a quick look
exercise: How many high tides are there roughly every day? | Two | One | Four | None, tides happen once a month
exercise: What mainly causes the tides? | The Moon's gravity | The wind blowing the sea | The Earth's magnetic field | The Sun's heat warming the water
exercise: When do spring tides happen? | At new and full Moon | Only in March, April and May | At the quarter Moons | Only during eclipses
exercise: When do neap tides happen? | At the quarter Moons | At full Moon | At new Moon | Only in autumn and winter
exercise: Which tides have the biggest range? | Spring tides | Neap tides | Winter tides | Night tides
exercise: Where are some of the biggest tides in the world? | The Bristol Channel and the Bay of Fundy | The Mediterranean Sea | Lake Victoria | The middle of the Pacific Ocean, far from land
test: teacher drew the phases as the Earth's shadow creeping over the Moon. is that right?? | No, phases come from seeing more or less of the Moon's sunlit half | Yes, that is exactly how phases work | Yes, but only for the full Moon | No, phases are caused by clouds
test: Your friend says there's a solar eclipse at full Moon tonight. What is wrong? | A solar eclipse can happen only at new Moon | Nothing, solar eclipses happen at full Moon | Solar eclipses happen only at midnight | Eclipses happen only in winter
test: The Moon looked like a thin smile lit on the right last night in Manchester. What will it do over the next week? | Grow, because it is waxing | Shrink, because it is waning | Stay the same for a month | Disappear and never come back
test: Harry plans to watch the eclipse with his mum's dark sunglasses. What should you tell him? | Not to: only proper eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector are safe | That is fine if he blinks a lot | Sunglasses are fine for a partial eclipse | Fine, if he looks only for a minute
test: If the Moon's orbit were not tilted at all, what would happen? | There would be a solar and a lunar eclipse every month | There would be no eclipses at all, not ever again, anywhere on Earth | There would be no phases | There would be no tides
test: Why can a Moon in a lunar eclipse look red instead of disappearing? | Sunlight bent through the Earth's air reaches it, like sunsets | The Moon heats up and glows red | Mars shines its red light on it | The Moon's volcanoes erupt during eclipses
test: Gran: "It's March, so we'll get spring tides at the beach." What is the truth? | Spring tides come at every new and full Moon, all year round | She is right, spring tides come only in spring | Spring tides come only in March | Spring tides are another word for floods
test: You go rockpooling at the Bristol Channel at a full Moon. What tide range should you expect? | A very big one, because it is a spring tide | A very small one, because it is a neap tide | No tide at all | The same as on any other day
test: A cartoon shows the sea going in only once a month. What is right? | Most coasts have two high tides roughly every day | Once a month is right | Once a year only, in the summer, when the sea is at its warmest | Only when it is windy
test: In a lunar eclipse, where is the Earth? | Between the Sun and the Moon | Behind the Moon | Between the Moon and Mars | On the far side of the Sun, hidden
test: The Moon is 400 times smaller than the Sun but about 400 times closer. What does this neat coincidence allow? | The Moon can just cover the Sun in a total solar eclipse | The Moon makes its own light | The Moon is the same size as the Sun | The Moon can block the Earth's shadow from ever reaching the Sun
test: mum: "tide's out, the Moon's not even up, so how's it the Moon's doing??" Best answer? | The Moon's pull works all the time, whether or not you can see it | Then it must be the wind doing it | Tides stop when the Moon has set | It is the Sun's heat drawing water away
exam: Identify the cause of the phases of the Moon. | The varying proportion of the sunlit hemisphere visible from the Earth | The progressive encroachment of the Earth's shadow | The variation in the light emitted by the Moon | The periodic obscuration of the Moon by clouds
exam: Ascertain the phase of the Moon at which a solar eclipse may occur. | New Moon | Full Moon | First quarter | Last quarter
exam: State the principal reason for which eclipses do not occur in every lunar month. | The orbit of the Moon is inclined by some five degrees to that of the Earth | The Moon is of insufficient size in most months | The Sun moves out of alignment each week | The Earth rotates too rapidly for alignment
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises spring tides? | Tides of greatest range, occurring at new and full Moon throughout the year | Tides occurring solely in the season of spring | Tides of least range, occurring at the quarter Moons in every month of the year | Tides caused principally by the action of the wind
